# NAV Analytics MCP server — environment template.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in real values. .env is gitignored (it holds a live
# Azure Storage key); this file is the committed, secret-free reference.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# In production these are App Service application settings, not a file.
# Every var below is optional — the defaults shown run the server against the
# local synthetic fixture with no credentials at all.


# ── Data source ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Where server.py reads NAV data from. Defaults point at the local parquet
# fixture generated by `python make_sample_data.py` (synthetic random-walk
# NAVs — wiring validation only, never real fund performance).
#
# Leave these unset (or as-is) for `python demo.py`.

NAV_HISTORY_PATH=./data/nav_history.parquet
SCHEME_MASTER_PATH=./data/scheme_master.parquet

# For real SIF data, point them at the Blob URIs instead and set the connection
# string below. This is the layout in sif/config/constants.py — the container is
# sifnavdata, and nav_history is partitioned by year:
#
#   NAV_HISTORY_PATH=az://sifnavdata/processed/nav_history/year=*/*.parquet
#   SCHEME_MASTER_PATH=az://sifnavdata/processed/scheme_master.parquet
#
# Note: mcp_client_test.py overrides both of these with the az:// URIs above,
# so it always tests against real Blob data regardless of what is set here.


# ── Azure Blob credentials ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# REQUIRED for az:// paths and for mcp_client_test.py, which exits immediately
# if it is missing. Not needed for demo.py / the local fixture.
#
# Same storage account as the MF pipeline — only the container differs. Get it
# from: Azure Portal → Storage account → Access keys → Connection string.
#
# This is a live secret. Put the real value in .env, never here.

AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING=DefaultEndpointsProtocol=https;AccountName=<account>;AccountKey=<key>;EndpointSuffix=core.windows.net

# Where blobs are cached locally after download. server.py fetches parquet via
# the Azure SDK (which verifies TLS against certifi) rather than DuckDB's azure
# extension, which fails on locked-down hosts like App Service, then hands
# DuckDB plain local files.
#
# The /tmp default is fine on Linux/containers. On Windows, set it to a real
# path — /tmp does not exist there:
#
#   BLOB_CACHE_DIR=./.cache/navdata

# BLOB_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/navdata


# ── Transport ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# "http" (default) serves the MCP endpoint at /mcp on 0.0.0.0:$PORT — this is
# what App Service and `docker run` use. "stdio" is for local MCP clients that
# spawn the process directly (Cursor, Claude Desktop).
#
# mcp_client_test.py passes stdio itself via the subprocess env, so it does not
# depend on this being set.

# MCP_TRANSPORT=http

# Listen port for http transport. App Service injects $PORT at runtime and
# overrides whatever is set here; the Dockerfile defaults it to 8000.

# PORT=8000


# ── OAuth (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Authentication is OFF unless PUBLIC_BASE_URL is set. Leave all four unset for
# local development and stdio clients.
#
# Setting PUBLIC_BASE_URL turns OAuth ON — and once it is on, OAUTH_CLIENT_ID
# and OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET become mandatory: server.py exits(1) at startup if
# either is missing.
#
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL is the externally reachable https origin of the deployed
# server, with no trailing slash and no /mcp suffix.

# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://your-app.azurewebsites.net
# OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=<client-id>
# OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=<client-secret>

# Space-separated allowed redirect URIs. Defaults to the Claude.ai callback, so
# it only needs setting for a different client.

# OAUTH_REDIRECT_URIS=https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback


# ── Holdings enricher (holdings_enricher/) ─────────────────────────────────
#
# API key for mfapi.advisorkhoj.com, the source of fund holdings. Required by
# holdings_enricher/main.py, which pulls portfolios per category/month and
# writes data/fund_holdings.json for the MCP holdings tools.
#
# The server itself does NOT read this — it consumes the generated JSON, so a
# deployed server needs no API key. This is a build-time credential only.
#
# The key travels as a query parameter, so it appears in request URLs; the
# client masks it in logs and errors. Keep it out of committed files.

# API_KEY=<advisorkhoj-api-key>
